# Sidecar L4DEL annotations for google-deepmind/formal-conjectures (pinned checkout).
# Status statements follow the source's own @[category ...] attributes and docstrings only.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1.lean
@decl Erdos1.IsSumDistinctSet
-- L4DEL: title: Sum-distinct sets
-- L4DEL: intuition: A finite set of naturals up to N is sum-distinct when no two different subsets add up to the same total.
-- L4DEL: step: The set A must be contained in the interval from 1 to N.
-- L4DEL: step: The map sending each subset S of A (taken from A.powerset) to its sum must be injective.
-- L4DEL: term: sum-distinct set = a finite set of positive naturals bounded by N whose subsets all have different sums

@decl Erdos1.erdos_1
-- L4DEL: title: Erdos Problem 1: sum-distinct sets need N of order 2^n
-- L4DEL: intuition: The statement says there is no constant C with N at least C * 2^n for every sum-distinct set of size n inside 1..N, and the file marks this as solved.
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research solved, and the docstring says the original conjecture N >> 2^n is false because a machine-checked disproof builds sets with N / 2^n tending to zero.
-- L4DEL: step: The formal statement is the negation of "there is C > 0 such that for all N and every sum-distinct A the inequality C * 2^|A| < N holds (for N not zero)".
-- L4DEL: step: In this repository the proof body is sorry; the attribute points to an external formal proof at the URL it cites.
-- L4DEL: prereq: Erdos1.IsSumDistinctSet

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/3.lean
@decl Erdos3.erdos_3
-- L4DEL: title: Erdos Problem 3: divergent reciprocal sums and arithmetic progressions
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the reciprocals of a set of naturals sum to infinity, must the set contain arithmetic progressions of every length? The file marks this as open.
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research open, and the statement is written as "answer(sorry) iff ...", so the yes-or-no answer itself is left unfilled.
-- L4DEL: step: The right-hand side says: for every set A of naturals whose reciprocal sum is not summable, there are arbitrarily large k (infinitely often in k) with a subset S of A that is an arithmetic progression of length k.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof body is sorry, as for the other open problems in the repository.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/CollatzConjecture.lean
@decl CollatzConjecture.collatzStep
-- L4DEL: title: One step of the Collatz map
-- L4DEL: intuition: Halve an even number, and triple an odd number then add one.
-- L4DEL: step: If n is even the result is n / 2 (natural-number division).
-- L4DEL: step: Otherwise the result is 3 * n + 1.

@decl CollatzConjecture.collatz_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: The Collatz conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: Starting from any positive integer and repeating the Collatz step, you eventually reach 1; the file marks this as open.
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research open, so the proof body is sorry.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement quantifies over every n greater than 0 and asserts some m with collatzStep iterated m times on n equal to 1.
-- L4DEL: prereq: CollatzConjecture.collatzStep
-- L4DEL: term: Collatz iteration = repeated application of collatzStep, written collatzStep^[m] n in Lean

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/TwinPrimes.lean
@decl TwinPrimes.twin_primes
-- L4DEL: title: The twin prime question
-- L4DEL: intuition: Are there infinitely many primes p for which p + 2 is also prime? The file marks this as open.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement has the form "answer(sorry) iff the set of p with p and p + 2 prime is infinite", so the answer slot is left unfilled.
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research open and the proof body is sorry.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/GoldbachConjecture.lean
@decl GoldbachConjecture.goldbach
-- L4DEL: title: Goldbach's conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: Can every even number greater than 2 be written as a sum of two primes? The file marks this as open.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement is "answer(sorry) iff for every even n greater than 2 there are primes p and q with n = p + q".
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research open and the proof body is sorry.
-- L4DEL: see: TernaryGoldbachConjecture.ternaryGoldbach

@decl TernaryGoldbachConjecture.ternaryGoldbach
-- L4DEL: title: Ternary Goldbach
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every odd number greater than 5 is a sum of three primes (primes may repeat); the file marks this as solved.
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research solved, and the docstring credits Harald Helfgott while noting the solution is not yet peer-reviewed in a journal.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement is a plain theorem with no answer slot; the proof body here is sorry.
-- L4DEL: see: GoldbachConjecture.goldbach

@file FormalConjectures/Millennium/RiemannHypothesis.lean
@decl RiemannHypothesis.riemannHypothesis
-- L4DEL: title: The Riemann Hypothesis
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every non-trivial zero of the Riemann zeta function has real part one half; the file marks this as open.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement is Mathlib's RiemannHypothesis proposition: for every complex s with zeta s = 0, s not of the form -2(n+1), and s not 1, the real part of s is 1/2.
-- L4DEL: step: The docstring describes it as the Clay Millennium Prize Problem; the attribute reads category research open and the proof body is sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: trivial zero = a zero of the zeta function at a negative even integer, excluded from the hypothesis

@file FormalConjectures/Millennium/PvsNP.lean
@decl ComplexityTheory.P_ne_NP
-- L4DEL: title: P is not equal to NP
-- L4DEL: intuition: The complexity classes P and NP differ; the file marks this as open.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement is the inequality of two language classes, P and NP, defined elsewhere in the repository's utility library.
-- L4DEL: step: The attribute reads category research open and the proof body is sorry.
-- L4DEL: see: ComplexityTheory.NP_ne_coNP
